content.text:
Done. All 6 tests green, no refactor needed — the implementation is already at minimum.

**`src/utils.js`** — `validateEmail(email)`:
```js
function validateEmail(email) {
  if (typeof email !== 'string') {
    return false;
  }

  const atIndex = email.indexOf('@');
  if (atIndex < 1) {
    return false;
  }
  return email.slice(atIndex + 1).includes('.');
}
```

**`test/validate-email.test.js`** — one test per behavior, each watched fail before the code existed:

| Test | Red failure seen |
|---|---|
| accepts `user@example.com` | `validateEmail is not a function` |
| rejects `user.example.com` (no `@`) | `true !== false` |
| rejects `@example.com` (empty local part) | `true !== false` |
| rejects `user@example` (no dot in domain) | `true !== false` |
| rejects `''` | passed on arrival — regression guard, not a new behavior |
| rejects `undefined` | `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` |

**Infrastructure added:** the repo had no test setup, so I wired `"test": "node --test"` into `package.json` using Node's built-in runner — keeps the project dependency-free and matches its CommonJS style.

**Two things to flag:**

- I added the non-string guard beyond your four rules. `validateEmail(undefined)` threw a `TypeError` rather than returning a boolean, which contradicts "return true/false" for a realistic call (an unset form field). If you'd rather it throw on bad input, say so and I'll drop the guard and its test.
- The check is deliberately literal to your spec, so `a@b@c.com` returns `true` (first `@`, domain `b@c.com` has a dot), and so does `user@.com`. If you want multiple-`@` rejection or a non-empty label before the dot, those are additional rules — tell me and I'll TDD them in.

Changes are uncommitted; run `git diff` to review.
